content.text:
Title generation now writes an `llm_inference` row, so its token usage/cost is tracked like memory extraction and compaction.

The changes in `apps/backend/src/services/agent.ts`:

```745:762:apps/backend/src/services/agent.ts
		const { text, usage } = await generateText({
			...disableModelReasoning(provider, modelResult),
			system: 'Generate a short, descriptive title (3-8 words) ...',
			// ...
		});

		this._trackTitleGenerationInference(summaryModelId, convertToTokenUsage(usage));
```

```778:788:apps/backend/src/services/agent.ts
	private _trackTitleGenerationInference(modelId: string, usage: TokenUsage): void {
		scheduleSaveLlmInferenceRecord({
			type: 'title_generation',
			projectId: this.chat.projectId,
			userId: this.chat.userId,
			chatId: this.chat.id,
			llmProvider: this._modelSelection.provider,
			llmModelId: modelId,
			...usage,
		});
	}
```

A few notes on the choices. The `title_generation` value already existed in `LLM_INFERENCE_TYPES`, so no schema or migration change was needed — nothing had been writing it until now. I used `scheduleSaveLlmInferenceRecord` (like `compaction.ts` does) rather than awaiting the insert, so a DB hiccup can't break title generation. The record is written right after the model call and before the `sanitizeTitle` early-return, so the cost is captured even when the model returns an unusable title and we fall back to `titleFromPrompt`. The logged model is `summaryModelId`, the annotation model actually resolved for the call, not the chat's main model.

One thing worth flagging: the usage settings page and `usage.queries.ts` don't read from `llm_inference` at all — only `app-db-views.ts` exposes it. So these rows are recorded but won't show up in the usage UI until something queries them.
